  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nIsaias will continue to move east into tonight. Precipitation\nand gusty winds will subside as generalized ascent and gradient\nwinds move with it. Precipitation chances will last the longest\nsoutheast of Pittsburgh into the high terrain with orographic \nlift and greatest proximity to coastal low development, but \nmedian 24 rain through 2pm Monday sits only around an additional\n0.1 to 0.3 inches with a worst-scae scenario bulls-eye of 1.5\" \nshould showers form tonight. Even this would likely not lead to any \nadditional flood concerns. As for wind, gusts up to 30-40mph in high \nterrain this afternoon will calm to light northwest winds tonight, \nso no additional concerns there as well. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nInto mid-week, the 500mb pattern trends more towards a western \ntrough and an eastern ridge, which will actually push \ntemperatures close to 15 degrees above normal Tuesday. A low \npassing to the north will accelerate gradient low-level flow \nTuesday, though LREF keeps a 90% chance of SBCAPE >500, and a \n50% chance of values near 0. SPC has hoisted a Day-3 Marginal \nRisk in north central PA for now where shear is maximized (near\n40kts), with damaging wind as the primary threat.\n\nBetter rain chances arrive into Wednesday as a weak shortwave\nmoves through, with QPF totals anywhere from a light\naccumulation to 0.50\" (NBM 25th-90th), should the shortwave \nmaterialize.\n\nThe big question then becomes what will happen with a late-week\nsystem, of which, ensembles are still highly uncertain. The main\ndriver of uncertainty is the timing and placement of a digging\neastern tough. In scenario 1, the trough digs east of the area \nsometime Thursday thru Saturday. In this scenario, the area \nwould be warmer than ensemble means and less unsettled. In \nscenario 2, the trough barrels down directly into the area with\na strong cold front (favored Friday into Saturday) and \npotentially bring some rain, winds (approaching advisory \ncriteria, at minimum), and even lake- enhanced snow/rain shower\npotential into Saturday. In scenario 3, the trough may drop to \ninto the Midwest sometime Friday/Saturday with rain ahead of it,\nand bring a longer period of cool and windy conditions as the \nupper low struggles to rejoin the flow into next weekend. If\ntruly maximized, scenario 2 would be the most impactful due to \nwind gusts risks and lake effect snow potential (2-10% \nprobability for locations to see >= 3\" of snow).",
